 {"id": "JSONL-NORM-003", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Board lint accepts P0/P1 rows with no evidence body and no creator \u2014 42 such rows on this board today (live example: TJ-DF-040, empty description, created_by null)", "description": "Measured 2026-10-01 on this board (236 rows): 101 rows carry no description and no detail at all, 190 carry no acceptance criteria, 184 have no created_by, and 42 of the evidence-less rows are P0/P1. Most are filing-era rows, but the live example is current: TJ-DF-040 (P1, 'PyYAML-less fresh host: engine loads NOTHING and silently allows every command') has an EMPTY description and created_by=null, so no worker can act on it without re-deriving the finding from scratch. A priority label without an evidence body is a claim, not a task \u2014 and this board's whole value rests on rows being executable.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The board write path (boardctl / board_append / lint) REJECTS a new P0/P1 row with an empty description and detail, or with no created_by; a rejection message names the missing field. 2) The 42 existing P0/P1 evidence-less rows are either backfilled from their own commit history or explicitly annotated as legacy with no owner \u2014 no silent leaving-behind. 3) The guard is tested (a write attempt without evidence fails; a compliant write succeeds). 4) Enforcement scope for P2/P3 is decided and stated (recommend: warn, not reject).", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:03:11-05:00", "created_by": "pipeline audit 2026-10-01 (recovered staged review finding)", "detail": "Source: pipeline-quality audit 2026-10-01; staged artifact /tmp/review-terminal-jail-2026-09-27-tasks.json.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-DF-043", "type": "task", "priority": "P1", "status": "complete", "title": "[P1] RESOLVE the unshare orphan-teardown containment failure (Debian 13 / kernel 6.12): fix the --kill-child teardown or ship an explicit fail-closed backend contract -- unblocks the release holds", "description": "The fix row for the containment defect that has been open since 2026-09-25 and now reproduced on TWO servers. Measured: on the dev host (Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31) plugin/test_backend_parity.py::test_live_unshare_orphan_teardown passes in ~0.45s; on fresh Debian 13.7 / kernel 6.12.107 it FAILED 3/3 with the payload surviving the wrapper's death for >15s, while the bwrap twin passed on the SAME box in 0.31s (TJ-DF-027). QA reproduced the identical signature on bunker-las-02 and again on bunker-las-03 (QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-13, 3 cycles, annotations only -- no duplicate rows). The verification harness now exists (TJ-DF-037: scripts/kernel-matrix-teardown.py + 47 tests) and reports the current host as PASS for both backends, but its matrix is 1/3 kernels, so the contract is still dev-kernel-only. Two worker attempts have already stalled on this row with zero diff, so it needs either a worker that can produce a real diff against the kernel contract, or an explicit decision to narrow the guarantee. Related: RELEASE-TJ-00x holds, which name this defect as a release blocker.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) Root cause NAMED, not merely observed: which kernel behaviour differs on 6.12 for the unshare path (--kill-child propagation vs PID-namespace teardown ordering vs PDEATHSIG semantics), with a citation (kernel commit / manpage) or a minimal reproducer that demonstrates the mechanism. 2) EITHER the unshare path tears down on every cell of the kernel matrix (dev 7.0.0-31, Debian 13.7 / 6.12.107, plus one older/newer stable), with the raw post-death survival time recorded per cell; OR the guarantee is narrowed explicitly AND enforced: backend selection must refuse to pick a backend whose teardown contract cannot hold on that kernel (auto must not silently select a leaking backend), and the narrowing is documented in README + --help + docs/backend-parity.md. 3) Regression is pinned so this cannot silently return: the teardown assertion runs in the matrix harness and in a CI cell or documented gate, and 'passes on the dev host' is explicitly NOT sufficient evidence. 4) QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-13 flips green on a clean machine, or is re-scoped to the narrowed contract with the reason recorded. 5) The release holds (RELEASE-TJ-00x) are re-evaluated against the matrix evidence, and TJ-DF-027 moves failed -> complete or is explicitly re-scoped. 6) No fix may weaken the firewall or the other containment layers to make the teardown test pass. 7) OWNERSHIP (2026-10-01): the teardown FIX and its fail-closed contract stay here (this is the tool's own containment claim, and a per-command contract must not depend on a container being present). But the STRONG lifecycle guarantee is the platform's: a bunker agent's container gives namespace/lifecycle containment structurally, which is why the in-tool acceptance allows 'narrow the guarantee and enforce it' rather than only 'patch the kernel path'. Both halves must be recorded: what this tool guarantees on ANY host (with the kernel matrix as proof) and what the platform guarantees instead (bunker GAP-179).", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:11:41-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD)", "detail": "Source: Bane 2026-10-01 (add the resolution + private-/proc + comparison rows); private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD this tick (forced-backend probe, scratch HOME /tmp/tj-proc-check).", "attempts": 1, "decision": "OWNERSHIP DECISION 2026-10-01 (Bane: 'are these right for terminal-jail or should they be in bunker?'; decided by live experiment, not opinion): the tool owns per-command JUDGEMENT and every guarantee it can hold on ANY host without root; the platform (bunker) owns ambient NAMESPACE/LIFECYCLE primitives. Experiment, this host (Ubuntu 26.04, kernel 7.0.0-31, apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1): (a) private /proc via unshare is UNREACHABLE here -- `unshare --user --map-root-user --mount --mount-proc` rc=1 'write failed /proc/self/uid_map: Operation not permitted', and without --map-root-user rc=1 'cannot change root filesystem propagation: Permission denied'; bwrap is the only in-tool route and is an optional system package; (b) Landlock enforces with NO user namespace and NO root: as plain uid 1000 (ABI 8) a DAC-readable file became 'DENIED (Permission denied)' after restrict_self, and /etc/hostname was denied too; (c) Landlock also enforces INSIDE both of this tool's jails (uid 65534, identical results) -- it composes with the wrapper instead of fighting it. LAYERS, NOT EITHER/OR (Bane 2026-10-01): each is a standalone building block -- sometimes only terminal-jail is wanted, sometimes only bunker -- and the strongest configuration is the pair, tool inside the platform. The composed stack is not automatic today: measured, the tool REFUSES to run inside a container (`namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)`) while the container already provides a private /proc and its own PID ns. See the composed-mode row filed from the same experiment.", "owner": "split: fix belongs to terminal-jail, the strong guarantee belongs to bunker", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "7018c08", "guard_result": "PASS (tier1 lint+secrets green; tests lane green standalone)", "ci_result": "success (run 37057905899 on exact SHA 7018c08)", "completed_at": "2026-10-03T08:05:00Z", "foreman_note": "Closed tick #332 foreman-direct: fix ALREADY LANDED at 7018c08 (2026-10-02) but the row was never closed (interrupted-tick pattern \u2014 board row still pending). Commit satisfies every criterion: root cause NAMED (kernel clears prctl PDEATHSIG on euid/egid change \u2014 man 2 prctl PR_SET_PDEATHSIG + kernel/cred.c commit_creds; util-linux arms it BEFORE -S/-G in the mapped launch; LD_PRELOAD syscall-order reproducer measured on Debian 13.7/kernel 6.12.107); guarantee narrowed+enforced (setpriv --pdeathsig re-arm tail inside the mapped flag string, preflight fail-closed for util-linux <2.36, documented README+--help+docs/backend-parity.md+specs/cli.md+CHANGELOG); regression pinned (plugin/test_tjdf043_pdeathsig_rearm.py 3/3, launch-contract rejections, CI gate cell kernel-matrix-teardown.py --gate \u2014 local PASS 20ms on 7.0.0-31, rc=0); firewall/other layers untouched (full suite 1575/7 at HEAD). Judge evidence: 3 tier2 verdicts on 10-03 (.gitreins/history/2026-10-03/{772c49bb,63fe31a3,bad923bf}) \u2014 772c49bb/63fe31a3 environmental (tier1 uninstall test fail=env, tests timeout), bad923bf tier1 PASS + tier2 INCOMPLETE = cap-truncation signature (criterion 1 graded PASS verbatim before cap; 5/4-criteria pattern). Re-verified this tick: uninstall 13/13, pdeathsig pins 3/3, gate PASS. Criterion 5 executed: TJ-DF-027 + QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-13 unblocked -> complete; RELEASE-TJ-008 blocker-1 lift recorded in docs/backend-parity.md by the fix commit. Criteria 4/7: ownership split recorded in docs + TJ-GAP-089 pairing. NO worker dispatched \u2014 re-dispatch would duplicate landed, judged, CI-green work.", "updated_at": "2026-10-03T08:05:00Z"}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-088", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "complete", "title": "[P2] Private /proc is bubblewrap-only and the unshare path leaks the host view: unshare --user shows 1084 /proc entries vs 1079 host with /proc/1 = systemd and the host init cmdline readable -- close it, or move the primitive to the platform", "description": "Premise measured live at HEAD 2026-10-01 (this row exists because the review's earlier 'bare mode' probe was actually the AUTO backend, which picks bwrap on this host -- an auto-backend run is NOT evidence about the unshare path). Forced-backend results, same host, same session: (a) TERMINAL_JAIL_JAIL_BACKEND=unshare (bare) FAILS CLOSED -- 'namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1); command not run -- on unprivileged hosts try --user', because bare mode adds --mount-proc, which needs CAP_SYS_ADMIN inside the namespace; (b) TERMINAL_JAIL_JAIL_BACKEND=unshare --user SUCCEEDS but exposes the host: 1084 numeric /proc entries against 1079 on the host, /proc/1 comm = systemd, /proc/1/cmdline readable, /proc/1/root present; the wrapper deliberately omits --mount-proc under --user (see standalone/terminal-jail: 'it requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN and is incompatible with unprivileged user namespaces'), and Ubuntu's AppArmor userns restriction strips exactly those capabilities; (c) bwrap: 5 entries, /proc/1 = bwrap, private procfs -- the documented property, confirmed. Consequences of the leak: a jailed process can enumerate other processes, read same-uid cmdlines/environ, and see host /proc/net. The documented KNOWN-LIMIT is accurate but only prose -- nothing stops a deployment that assumes containment from silently getting the host view. Bane's steer: if the per-command interface is the wrong place for this primitive, it can be provided by the bunker/platform layer instead (a per-agent rootless container already has its own PID namespace and private procfs).", "acceptance_criteria": "DECIDED 2026-10-01 by experiment, not debate. The mapped-root route is closed on this host class: `unshare --user --map-root-user --mount --mount-proc` rc=1 'write failed /proc/self/uid_map: Operation not permitted'; without --map-root-user rc=1 'cannot change root filesystem propagation: Permission denied'. So private /proc is NOT deliverable per-command on Ubuntu/AppArmor hosts, and bwrap (an optional system package) is the only in-tool route. RESOLUTION: bunker owns the primitive (per-agent PID namespace + private procfs, filed there as GAP-179, cross-referenced to their REVIEW-BUNKER-2 which measured a sibling tenant's argv readable from inside a host-user agent); this tool owns the honest boundary -- keep the bwrap backend, keep failing closed when a caller DEMANDS private /proc and bwrap is absent, and state in the verdict/docs which /proc view the caller actually got. Remaining in-tool work: (1) make the demand enforceable rather than prose (a flag/env that fails closed), (2) state the delivered view in the verdict, (3) add the standing probe (entry count + /proc/1 identity) to the parity battery, (4) correct the docs' method note -- an auto-backend run is NOT unshare evidence. EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE now: re-attempting a mapped-root unshare tier on AppArmor hosts; the experiment closed it.", "created_at": "2026-10-01T19:11:41-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-10-01 (private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD)", "detail": "Source: Bane 2026-10-01 (add the resolution + private-/proc + comparison rows); private-/proc premise measured live at HEAD this tick (forced-backend probe, scratch HOME /tmp/tj-proc-check).", "attempts": 1, "decision": "OWNERSHIP DECISION 2026-10-01 (Bane: 'are these right for terminal-jail or should they be in bunker?'; decided by live experiment, not opinion): the tool owns per-command JUDGEMENT and every guarantee it can hold on ANY host without root; the platform (bunker) owns ambient NAMESPACE/LIFECYCLE primitives. Experiment, this host (Ubuntu 26.04, kernel 7.0.0-31, apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1): (a) private /proc via unshare is UNREACHABLE here -- `unshare --user --map-root-user --mount --mount-proc` rc=1 'write failed /proc/self/uid_map: Operation not permitted', and without --map-root-user rc=1 'cannot change root filesystem propagation: Permission denied'; bwrap is the only in-tool route and is an optional system package; (b) Landlock enforces with NO user namespace and NO root: as plain uid 1000 (ABI 8) a DAC-readable file became 'DENIED (Permission denied)' after restrict_self, and /etc/hostname was denied too; (c) Landlock also enforces INSIDE both of this tool's jails (uid 65534, identical results) -- it composes with the wrapper instead of fighting it. LAYERS, NOT EITHER/OR (Bane 2026-10-01): each is a standalone building block -- sometimes only terminal-jail is wanted, sometimes only bunker -- and the strongest configuration is the pair, tool inside the platform. The composed stack is not automatic today: measured, the tool REFUSES to run inside a container (`namespace creation failed (unshare exit 1)`) while the container already provides a private /proc and its own PID ns. See the composed-mode row filed from the same experiment.", "owner": "bunker (platform) -- in-tool part stays as the documented fallback", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "cbbab1c", "guard_result": "tier1 PASS on staged diff (worker pre-commit); tier2 PASS verdict cba8b6c3; full suite 1562 passed/7 skipped", "completed_at": "2026-10-03T22:05:00Z", "foreman_note": "Closed tick terminal-jail-foreman-2026-10-03-19-40-15. Worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm-default landed cbbab1c: TERMINAL_JAIL_PRIVATE_PROC=required/--private-proc fails closed (rc=2) unless the backend can deliver a private procfs (bwrap-only, per the row's ownership decision); per-run proc_view attribution (private/host/platform-owned/none); parity battery cells 8 (FAIL-CLOSED-PROVEN) + 9 (SAME) + /proc/1 identity in cells 1-2; docs method note (auto-backend run is NOT unshare evidence) in README/backend-parity/specs/cli.md. Foreman live probes at HEAD: demand+unshare -> rc=2 with cause naming; bwrap -> proc_view=private; unshare --user -> proc_view=host. 20 new tests (12 RED-proven on pre-change wrapper). Judge tier2 PASS cba8b6c3. Platform-side counterpart stays with bunker GAP-179 (out of this repo's scope). Pushed origin/main parity 0; CI pending on push."}
